  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 AM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Above normal temperatures are expected through \nFriday. \n\nA moisture-starved cold front will move through the region tonight \ninto early Friday. Ahead of the front, weak downslope winds and \nrising low-level thicknesses will propel afternoon highs some 6-10 \ndegrees above normal in the lower to middle 80s.\n\nBehind the front, cooler high pressure settles in Friday, gradually \nstrengthening into Saturday over the Mid-Atlantic region. Northeast \nflow tomorrow will promote lower daytime highs but still a few \ndegrees above average in the mid to upper 70s. Dewpoints will be a \ntouch higher today, but drier air works in behind the front Friday \nfor another comfortable afternoon as dewpoints drop into the 40s \nover most of central NC.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... The likelihood of unsettled weather and significant \nrain associated with Isaias continues to increase for late Saturday \nthrough early Sunday. Rapid clearing in the rear of the system \nSunday.\n\nTropical Storm Isaias is forecast to quickly strengthen into a \nhurricane on Thursday, move ashore on the northern Gulf Coast Friday \nnight near MS/AL/western FL, with the system center turning NNW \nSaturday and Saturday night. This is a bit west of the previous \ntrack forecasts as once the system weakens over land, it will get \npulled into the upper low over the mid-MS valley region then. \nFinally, the remnant low should turn NE through the OH Valley late \nin the weekend. \n\nThe timing of showers for central NC continues to focus on late \nSaturday into Saturday night and Sunday. We will likely have the \nlarge surface high shift east and off the New England coast Saturday \nnight and Sunday as the tropical cyclone weakens to a depression and \nremnant low. As this occurs, showers are expected over the far \nsouthern and western areas that will likely set up a CAD event by \nSaturday night. A coastal front is expected along the SE US, and \nthis will likely get pulled inland (NW) into SC and far SE NC on \nSaturday night. Central NC may have to rely on a secondary low \npressure to form along this boundary for the most significant rain \nlate Saturday night and Sunday morning. In an interesting twist, the \nNW Piedmont may again have a minimum in QPF with this event with the \nexpected CAD in that region. The ESE upslope flow into the Blue \nRidge should lead to a maximum there. And, the coastal front's \nsecondary low should lead to a maximum of QPF over the Coastal Plain \nand maybe the eastern Piedmont. Overall, 0.5 to 1 inch storm totals \nin the NW Piedmont and 1-2 inches elsewhere. \n\nShowers should taper off and end quickly on Sunday with the dry slot \nand the sinking/downslope flow in the rear of the system as it \ndeparts to our N-NE. Sunshine and warmth will likely return \nlater Sunday.",
